---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
const unified = process.env.PIT_UNIFIED_PREVIEW === "1";
---
<Layout title="How PIT works" description="Understand free tools, local operations, wallet permissions, fees and privacy before using PIT.">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">PIT</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Guide</span></nav>
  <section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Getting started</div><h1>Use the right PIT surface for your task.</h1><p>PIT separates browser-local analysis, explicit public-chain reads and wallet-confirmed operations. Each has a different privacy and risk boundary.</p></section>
  <section class="product-lanes">
    <article class="panel"><h2>1. Free Toolbox</h2><p>Enter assumptions and calculate locally. No wallet or PIT account. Its isolated build blocks network connections. Downloaded files stay under your control.</p>{unified && <a href="/toolbox/">Open Toolbox →</a>}</article>
    <article class="panel"><h2>2. Read-only Explorer</h2><p>Start bounded blockchain queries yourself. Public RPC providers can observe submitted addresses. Results are snapshots, not complete ownership or security audits.</p>{unified && <a href="/explorer/">Open Explorer →</a>}</article>
    <article class="panel"><h2>3. Operations</h2><p>Configure an action, review exact values, simulate, approve in a wallet and verify its receipt. Current execution is confined to local Anvil chain 31337.</p><a href="/#directory">Browse operations →</a></article>
  </section>
  <section class="panel"><h2>Fees and gas</h2><p>Most PIT tools remain free. Some operations have proposed USDC service fees; these are inactive outside a local MOCK-USDC demonstration. Gas is paid separately in the chain's native ETH. PIT never adds ETH and USDC into a fabricated total or quotes a live exchange rate.</p><p>In the local atomic payment path, a reverted operation does not retain the service fee. A separate approval or reverted operation can still consume gas. A confirmed creation or distribution is generally irreversible. Direct calls to permissionless contracts remain possible outside PIT's guided path.</p></section>
  <section class="panel"><h2>Wallet and contract permissions</h2><p>Never provide a seed phrase or private key. Token creation roles go to the creator, not PIT. An ERC-20 allowance authorizes a specific spender up to its stated amount; review and revoke excess allowances. ERC-721 per-token approval differs from ERC-1155 collection-wide operator approval. Lockers and vesting contracts hold tokens on-chain and release them according to their code and schedule; beneficiary and funding semantics must be reviewed before signing.</p></section>
  <section class="panel"><h2>Data and metadata</h2><p>CSV and financial inputs remain client-side in the free tools and local operations where feasible. Public RPC queries reveal queried addresses to the selected provider. On-chain transactions reveal addresses and amounts publicly. PIT's NFT metadata studio prepares files locally; it does not host or pin images or JSON to IPFS.</p></section>
  <section class="panel"><h2>Not yet available</h2><p>No public paid checkout, mainnet transaction execution, merchant payout wallet or live premium-report sale is active. The x402 report experiment has simulated verification and settlement only. Real browser-extension wallet acceptance, independent contract audit, consumer terms, privacy notice and commercial identity review remain prerequisites for a later launch.</p>{unified && <p><a href="/toolbox/privacy/">Toolbox privacy page</a> · <a href="/toolbox/legal/">Site information</a></p>}</section>
</Layout>
